# Exercise 7 - Financial Forecasting

## 1. Understand Recursive Algorithms

Recursion is a programming technique in which a method calls itself to solve a problem.

A recursive solution mainly contains two parts:

- Base Case - Stops the recursive calls.
- Recursive Case - Calls the same method with a smaller version of the problem.

Recursion can simplify problems where the same calculation needs to be repeated multiple times.

In this exercise, recursion is used to calculate the future financial value by applying a growth rate for a given number of years.

## 2. Setup

A C# Console Application named `FinancialForecasting` was created using Visual Studio.

The project contains the following files:

- `ForecastCalculator.cs` - Contains the recursive method to calculate the future value.
- `Program.cs` - Provides the financial data and tests the recursive method.

## 3. Implementation

A method named `CalculateFutureValue` was created to calculate the predicted future value.

The method accepts the following values:

- Current Value
- Growth Rate
- Number of Years

The recursive method applies the growth rate to the current value and decreases the number of years by one after each recursive call.

The recursion stops when the number of years becomes zero.

### Base Case

When the number of years becomes zero, the method returns the calculated current value.

### Recursive Case

For each recursive call, the growth rate is applied to the current value and the number of remaining years is reduced by one.

For example:

Current Value = 10000

Growth Rate = 10%

Number of Years = 3

The calculation is performed as follows:

Year 1 = 11000

Year 2 = 12100

Year 3 = 13310

Therefore, the predicted future value is 13310.

## 4. Analysis

### Time Complexity

The time complexity of the recursive algorithm is `O(n)`, where `n` represents the number of years.

This is because the method makes one recursive call for each year.

### Space Complexity

The space complexity is `O(n)` because each recursive call uses space in the call stack until the base case is reached.

### Optimization

The current recursive solution makes only one recursive call for each year and does not repeat the same calculations.

However, recursion uses additional call stack memory. For a very large number of years, an iterative approach can be used to reduce the space complexity from `O(n)` to `O(1)`.

Memoization can also be useful in recursive problems where the same calculations are repeated multiple times. It stores previously calculated results and helps avoid unnecessary computations.

### Conclusion

Recursion provides a simple way to calculate future values by repeatedly applying the growth rate until the required number of years is reached.

The implemented solution has a time complexity of `O(n)`. For a large number of calculations, an iterative or mathematical approach can be used for better space efficiency.